course: dictionary
lesson: 15090
title: Roommate
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A roommate is a person who shares a room with you. When you meet this word, look at the words around it. «At camp, my roommate and I shared a bunk bed.» The clues are shared and bunk bed: the roommate sleeps in the same room as you.
explain: In some countries, a roommate can also share a whole flat or house. «In the US, Sam moved into a flat with a roommate who loves cooking.» The clue is flat: the roommate lives with you, not only sleeps beside you. Living together is the big idea.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "a person who shares a room and I shared a bunk bed". It still makes sense, so your meaning fits. In the UK, people often say flatmate or housemate for the same idea.
fact: A roommate is a person who shares a bedroom with you.
fact: In the US and Canada, a roommate can also share a flat or house with you.
fact: In the UK, people often say flatmate or housemate instead.
fact: Roommates are common in camps, boarding schools and colleges.
fact: Useful phrases: «my roommate» and «share with a roommate».
exercise: «At camp, my roommate and I shared a bunk bed.» Who is a roommate? | A bus driver on the school trip | A friend who lives in another town | A teacher who checks the register | *A person who shares a room with you
exercise: «In college, Sam's roommate plays the drums late at night.» What does roommate mean here? | A teacher who lives at the college | A shop near the college gate | A kind of drum kit | *A person who shares a room or flat with Sam
exercise: «Mia and her roommate share a small kitchen in their flat.» Which word tells you that Mia and her roommate live together? | *share | kitchen | small | Mia
test: In the UK, what do people often say instead of roommate? | Classmate | Playmate | *Flatmate | Teammate
test: «Lena and her roommate wake up at seven every day.» What is the most likely meaning of roommate here? | *A person who shares a room with Lena | A person who works in the same office | A neighbour across the street | A friend who only visits at weekends
exam: Which sentence uses roommate correctly? | *«My roommate and I share a room at camp.» | «We roommate to the shop on Sunday.» | «The roommate sang a song at the park.» | «The roommate is a big, red bus.»
